# Technical refresh — 2026-02-14 09:10 UTC

## Purpose and evidence status

This refresh re-checks ... It is a planning finding, not an acceptance, order, commitment, or vendor selection. Canonical sources unchanged.

### Evidence now available

| Ref | Item | Status | What it says |
...
E0 accepted canonical ...
E1 2026-02-10 bulletin sha 0e997... UNACCEPTED ...
E2 2026-02-14 corrected bulletin sha 14ffd... UNACCEPTED ...
E3 2026-02-14 duplicate sha 0e997... UNACCEPTED; byte-identical to E1...
Solaris claim ... unaccepted/unverified...
Late report ... cross-check only...

The two candidate tables E1 and E2 conflict on exactly one cell: V4 coastal_warranty (no vs yes). Both agree V3 min is 0 C. E3 repeats E1's bytes. Under operations, E1, E2, E3 are separate candidates; acceptance names one exact digest. Neither the filename "corrected" nor later arrival makes E2 accepted. The duplicate does not corroborate correctness, only that the original bytes were delivered again. The late report predates E2 and only covers E0/E1; it is not authority and cannot settle the conflict. [Maybe "predates" is not exact; it was prepared during earlier observation, before E2 arrived. Fine.]

## Site findings

### Site A — Marsh Lane Store
Constraints...
Under E0, E1, E2: V1 and V4 comply (-20 C); V2 (-5) and V3 (0) fail -12. V4 width 850 irrelevant; shorter warranty 5 years is not a binding constraint. Historical V1 remains compliant. No change from refresh.

### Site B — Harbour Workshop
Constraints...
- E0: V3 (min -10, coastal yes) is the only compliant vendor.
- E1/E3: no compliant vendor; V3 min 0 fails -4; V1/V2/V4 coastal no.
- E2: V4 (min -20, coastal yes) is compliant; V3 min 0 fails. V4 width 850 is not constrained at B; its 5-year warranty is shorter than V3's 12 but R3 only requires coastal cover.
Finding: Site B is no longer a single "no compliant vendor" conclusion. It is evidence-dependent:
- if E2 accepted: V4 is a compliant candidate.
- if E1/E3 accepted (or their content treated as current): no compliant vendor.
- if none accepted: E0 remains canonical, so V3 is still the last accepted selection, but the conflicting supplier bulletins are a material procurement risk and V3 cannot be ordered on unverified 0 C data.
Do not treat E2 as accepted; do not order V4 or V3.
V2 fails B on coastal warranty (and 2023 cold-start note anyway below -5); certificate claim does not change V3's 0 C.
Maybe V2 min -5 meets -4, so only coastal fails; the 2023 note says do not specify V2 below -5, and B is -4, so the note doesn't disqualify it at -4; but coastal no does. Don't overcomplicate: "V2 fails the coastal-warranty condition; its -5 C rating would meet -4 C, but that is moot without coastal cover." Good.

### Site C — School Annexe
- E0/E1/E2: V2 (650, -5) and V3 (750, 0) comply; V1 900/V4 850 too wide. Both minima at or below 2 C. Historical V3; V2 also compliant. 2023 V2 cold-start failure applies below -5; C at 2 C, so not disqualifying. Changing C to V2 would be a separate selection and cost change.

## Decisive evidence question(s)
1. V3 min: E0 -10 vs E1/E2 0. If either candidate accepted, V3 not B-compliant.
2. V4 coastal: E0/E1 no vs E2 yes. This determines whether B has any compliant vendor under the candidate data. E2 is the only candidate that supplies a B-compliant vendor; E1/E3 leave B with none.
3. Which digest is accepted? E2 vs E1/E3. Both unaccepted; conflict unresolved. Acceptance of one does not accept the other. Duplicate adds no new evidence.
4. Certificate: still no document/number/body/standard; does not resolve.
